Q1: What is the advantage of abstract methods?
Abstract methods are useful because:
They enforce rules – subclasses must implement them.
They support polymorphism – one reference can call different implementations.
They provide code reusability & flexibility – common code stays in abstract class, specific code in subclasses.
They enable multiple inheritance (via interfaces) – a class can implement multiple contracts.

Q2: Why do abstract methods not have a body?
Because the purpose of an abstract method is not to provide implementation but to define a blueprint (contract).
The actual implementation is given by the subclasses that inherit it.

Q3.Why must a class containing an abstract method be declared abstract?
In Java, if a class contains an abstract method, it must be declared abstract because the class becomes
incomplete. Abstract methods don’t have a body, so objects of such a class cannot be created directly.
By declaring the class abstract, we make it clear that this class is only a blueprint, and its subclasses
are responsible for providing the actual implementation of the abstract methods.

Q4.Rule for Abstract Methods in Inheritance
If subclass is concrete → it must implement all abstract methods.
If subclass is also abstract → it can skip some (or all) implementations, and pass the responsibility further.

Q5.Can abstract methods be static in Java?
Abstract method → must be overridden.
Static method → cannot be overridden.(belongs to class itself, not to instances)
Therefore, abstract static method is not allowed in Java.

Q6.Why can’t we create objects of an abstract class?
We cannot create objects of an abstract class because an abstract class is incomplete.
It may contain abstract methods (methods without a body), which means it doesn’t provide full implementation.
If objects of an abstract class were allowed, we could end up calling abstract methods that have no definition —
which would lead to runtime errors. To prevent this, Java makes it a compile-time rule that you cannot instantiate an abstract class.
Abstract classes are meant to act as blueprints or base classes.
Their actual object is created through their concrete subclasses, which provide the missing implementations.


Q7.Can Abstract class be final ?
An abstract class cannot be final. Abstract means the class must be inherited, and final means it cannot be inherited.
Since these are contradictory, Java does not allow an abstract final class.

Q8.Why can’t we achieve multiple inheritance using abstract classes, even though they also contain abstract methods like interfaces?

   Abstract class still allows implementation
   •	Abstract classes can have both abstract methods (without body) and concrete methods (with body).
   •	If Java allowed multiple inheritance of abstract classes, then there could be a diamond problem (method body conflict).

Q9.Why are variables in an interface always public static final in Java?
All variables in an interface are implicitly public static final because interfaces are meant to define constants,
not object states. static ensures the variable is shared, final ensures it cannot be changed, and public ensures it is accessible to all implementing classes.

Q10.Can methods in an interface be final in Java?
No, methods in an interface cannot be declared final. The purpose of an interface is to let implementing classes provide their own version of the method.
If a method were final, it couldn’t be overridden, which would completely contradict the idea of interfaces